(D) necessity and tightness
  (1) c <= tau: c = 1/2, tau = 1; the MAP is [(10, Fraction(0, 1))] although (R) holds with g = 0: the hypothesis c > tau is needed
  (2) eps = m_N(c) = 1: the near-optimal set [(5, Fraction(3, 1), 'M'), (2, Fraction(10, 1), 'N')] contains an N point: eps < m_N is needed
  (3) tightness: (R) holds with tau = 1, least g = 2; at c = 2 the MAPs are [(0, Fraction(10, 1)), (2, Fraction(6, 1))]; the longer one has l = 2 = l0 + g/(c - tau) = 2
  (4) without a right line (or with tau >= c) the MAP can be arbitrarily long: at c = 1/5 the MAP is [(40, Fraction(0, 1))]
